1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,975
The Pipelines That Feed Singapore

2
00:00:02,975 --> 00:00:07,382
On Saturday the 24th of
October 2026, Indonesia cut the

3
00:00:07,462 --> 00:00:12,113
natural gas it sends to Singapore
by pipeline, to keep its

4
00:00:12,193 --> 00:00:16,925
own power stations running
after a long blackout on Batam.

5
00:00:16,925 --> 00:00:19,925
In Singapore, nothing went dark.

6
00:00:19,925 --> 00:00:23,454
The Energy Market Authority
said the cut came to

7
00:00:23,534 --> 00:00:27,213
less than 2% of the gas
the country uses each day,

8
00:00:27,292 --> 00:00:30,450
and that there was enough gas to go round.

9
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:34,548
That it barely registered
goes back to three pipes

10
00:00:34,628 --> 00:00:37,975
laid under the sea between 1992 and 2003,

11
00:00:38,055 --> 00:00:42,400
and a backup built on Jurong
Island ten years later.

12
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:46,592
If you grew up in Singapore,
you probably think of the PUB as

13
00:00:46,672 --> 00:00:51,225
the people who look after the
water, the electricity and the gas.

14
00:00:51,225 --> 00:00:54,200
For more than three decades it was.

15
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,272
Today the system is split
between several organisations,

16
00:00:58,352 --> 00:01:00,650
and it works roughly like this.

17
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:04,156
Almost all of Singapore's electricity,

18
00:01:04,236 --> 00:01:08,200
about 95%, is made by burning natural gas.

19
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,425
Singapore has no gas of its own,
so every bit of it is imported.

20
00:01:13,425 --> 00:01:17,172
The gas goes to the power
stations, which are run

21
00:01:17,252 --> 00:01:20,062
by a handful of generating companies,

22
00:01:20,142 --> 00:01:24,750
among them Senoko Energy, YTL
PowerSeraya and PacificLight.

23
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:27,930
They sell their electricity
into a wholesale

24
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:30,900
market that is settled every half hour.

25
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:34,531
SP Group, the successor to
the PUB's power business,

26
00:01:34,611 --> 00:01:39,098
runs the national grid that carries
the power to every building,

27
00:01:39,178 --> 00:01:42,675
under a licence from the
Energy Market Authority.

28
00:01:42,675 --> 00:01:47,201
Households either buy from SP
Group at a regulated tariff or,

29
00:01:47,281 --> 00:01:49,693
since the Open Electricity Market

30
00:01:49,773 --> 00:01:53,700
reached the whole island
in 2018, choose a retailer.

31
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:56,196
The PUB itself has been the

32
00:01:56,276 --> 00:01:59,425
national water agency since 2001.

33
00:01:59,425 --> 00:02:03,313
Energy now has a ministry
in its name: from the 1st

34
00:02:03,393 --> 00:02:07,280
of October 2026, the Ministry
of Trade and Industry

35
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:11,250
became the Ministry of
Energy, Trade and Industry.

36
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:17,307
The PUB was set up on the 1st of May
1963 to take over the electricity,

37
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:21,450
gas and water services
of the old City Council.

38
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:25,863
For most of its history, its
power stations burned fuel oil,

39
00:02:25,943 --> 00:02:29,082
from the older stations
at Pasir Panjang to

40
00:02:29,162 --> 00:02:31,853
the offshore station on Pulau Seraya,

41
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:36,725
whose first oil-fired units came
into service in the late 1980s.

42
00:02:36,725 --> 00:02:39,385
Oil was easy to ship in and to store,

43
00:02:39,465 --> 00:02:44,500
and Singapore was already one of
the world's great refining centres.

44
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:47,336
But it was also expensive, and its price

45
00:02:47,416 --> 00:02:50,550
swung with every crisis
in the Middle East.

46
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:53,072
Natural gas burned more cleanly and

47
00:02:53,152 --> 00:02:56,125
could run more efficient power stations.

48
00:02:56,125 --> 00:02:58,422
The problem was getting it to an

49
00:02:58,502 --> 00:03:01,250
island with no gas fields of its own.

50
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:04,250
The answer came from Malaysia.

51
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:08,449
Petronas, Malaysia's national
oil company, was building the

52
00:03:08,529 --> 00:03:12,946
Peninsular Gas Utilisation system
to carry gas from its fields

53
00:03:13,026 --> 00:03:17,450
off the coast of Terengganu down
the length of the peninsula.

54
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:20,736
Its southern end reached
Pasir Gudang in Johor,

55
00:03:20,816 --> 00:03:24,389
and from there an undersea
line was laid across the

56
00:03:24,469 --> 00:03:28,050
Johor Strait to the PUB's
power station at Senoko.

57
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:33,496
Gas was due to start flowing
on the 29th of January 1992,

58
00:03:33,576 --> 00:03:37,550
about 150 million cubic feet of it a day.

59
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:41,575
A few years later the
PUB itself was broken up.

60
00:03:41,575 --> 00:03:46,520
On the 1st of October 1995, its
electricity and gas businesses were

61
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:51,545
turned into companies under a new
holding company, Singapore Power,

62
00:03:51,625 --> 00:03:56,275
owned by the government's
investment company Temasek Holdings.

63
00:03:56,275 --> 00:04:02,180
In 2001 the Energy Market Authority
was set up to regulate the industry,

64
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:06,250
and the PUB became the
water agency it is today.

65
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:10,600
The second source lay far
out in the South China Sea.

66
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:15,181
In the West Natuna fields, off
Indonesia's Natuna Islands,

67
00:04:15,261 --> 00:04:18,556
a group of oil companies led by Conoco and

68
00:04:18,636 --> 00:04:22,975
Indonesia's state oil company
Pertamina had found gas.

69
00:04:22,975 --> 00:04:26,671
In 1998 they agreed to sell it to SembGas,

70
00:04:26,751 --> 00:04:31,525
a consortium led by Singapore's
Sembawang Corporation:

71
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:35,300
325 million cubic feet a day for 22 years,

72
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:39,875
under a contract worth
about 8 billion US dollars.

73
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:44,705
To deliver it, the companies laid
a pipeline about 640 kilometres

74
00:04:44,785 --> 00:04:49,086
long across the seabed to
Singapore's waters, with a short

75
00:04:49,166 --> 00:04:53,850
final stretch to Jurong Island
and its petrochemical industry.

76
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:57,575
The gas began to flow in 2001.

77
00:04:57,575 --> 00:05:00,500
The third pipe came from the south.

78
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,291
In 2001 Singapore Power signed
an agreement with Pertamina,

79
00:05:05,371 --> 00:05:10,079
through its company Gas Supply
Private Limited, to buy gas

80
00:05:10,159 --> 00:05:14,949
from fields in South Sumatra,
starting at 150 million cubic

81
00:05:15,029 --> 00:05:19,900
feet a day and rising to 350
million by 2009, for 20 years.

82
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:24,768
The pipeline, a joint project of
Singapore Power's PowerGas and

83
00:05:24,848 --> 00:05:30,187
Indonesia's state gas company PGN,
runs about 470 kilometres from the

84
00:05:30,267 --> 00:05:35,450
gas plant at Grissik, by way of
the island of Batam, to Singapore.

85
00:05:35,450 --> 00:05:39,297
It was formally opened on
the 4th of August 2003,

86
00:05:39,377 --> 00:05:43,625
and the gas arrived about
a month later than planned.

87
00:05:43,625 --> 00:05:48,000
With three pipes running, gas
took over from oil very quickly.

88
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,913
In 2001 it made about a quarter

89
00:05:50,993 --> 00:05:53,600
of Singapore's electricity.

90
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,801
By 2003 it made more than half,

91
00:05:56,881 --> 00:05:59,950
and by 2014 almost all of it.

92
00:05:59,950 --> 00:06:02,435
By then, every molecule of that gas

93
00:06:02,515 --> 00:06:05,300
came by pipe from just two neighbours.

94
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:09,354
In 2006 the government decided
to build a terminal for

95
00:06:09,434 --> 00:06:13,181
liquefied natural gas, or
LNG: gas cooled until it

96
00:06:13,261 --> 00:06:17,238
turns to liquid, so that it
can be shipped across the

97
00:06:17,318 --> 00:06:21,375
world in tankers and turned
back into gas on arrival.

98
00:06:21,375 --> 00:06:26,432
The 1.7 billion Singapore
dollars terminal on Jurong Island

99
00:06:26,512 --> 00:06:31,300
took its first commercial
cargo on the 7th of May 2013.

100
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:34,000
The balance has shifted since.

101
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,471
In 2025, according to the
Energy Market Authority,

102
00:06:38,551 --> 00:06:45,205
about 57% of Singapore's gas imports
arrived as LNG from around the world,

103
00:06:45,285 --> 00:06:50,200
and about 43% by pipeline
from Malaysia and Indonesia.

104
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:54,981
Under an agreement made in
2022, Indonesia agreed to keep

105
00:06:55,061 --> 00:07:00,775
supplying gas by pipeline until
2028, at lower volumes than before.

106
00:07:00,775 --> 00:07:04,409
The cut announced on
the 24th of October was

107
00:07:04,489 --> 00:07:07,278
about 31 million cubic feet a day,

108
00:07:07,358 --> 00:07:12,000
according to Indonesia's
upstream regulator, SKK Migas.

109
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,783
Part of it came from the
Natuna line and part from

110
00:07:15,863 --> 00:07:19,414
the Sumatra line, and
the gas was to go instead

111
00:07:19,494 --> 00:07:23,125
to power stations on
Batam, Bintan and Sumatra.

112
00:07:23,125 --> 00:07:27,513
Earlier in the month a 16-hour
blackout on Batam had cut

113
00:07:27,593 --> 00:07:30,466
power to more than 411,000 customers,

114
00:07:30,546 --> 00:07:36,450
and Indonesia's government said it was
putting its own energy needs first.

115
00:07:36,450 --> 00:07:41,075
In Singapore, the Energy Market
Authority said the reduction

116
00:07:41,155 --> 00:07:45,153
reported by the importers,
expected to last 18 days,

117
00:07:45,233 --> 00:07:49,936
came to less than 2% of the
country's total daily gas demand,

118
00:07:50,016 --> 00:07:54,800
and that Singapore GasCo could
bring in more LNG at any time.

119
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:58,362
Prime Minister Lawrence
Wong said Singapore expected

120
00:07:58,442 --> 00:08:02,213
the commercial contracts to
be honoured, and pointed to

121
00:08:02,293 --> 00:08:06,075
the LNG terminal as the
reason the country could cope.

122
00:08:06,075 --> 00:08:08,373
Earlier in the year the system had

123
00:08:08,453 --> 00:08:10,900
already absorbed a different shock.

124
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:15,409
War in the Middle East had
halted production at Qatar's Ras

125
00:08:15,489 --> 00:08:20,231
Laffan gas plant since March,
though the authority said Qatari

126
00:08:20,311 --> 00:08:24,900
gas had met less than 10% of
Singapore's electricity needs.

127
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:28,384
Tan See Leng, the minister
in charge of energy,

128
00:08:28,464 --> 00:08:32,781
said electricity prices were
not expected to rise for now,

129
00:08:32,861 --> 00:08:36,425
and that a second LNG
terminal was being built.

130
00:08:36,425 --> 00:08:39,658
Further ahead, Singapore
is studying sources

131
00:08:39,738 --> 00:08:42,750
of power beyond gas, nuclear among them.

132
00:08:42,750 --> 00:08:46,544
In 2012 a government
pre-feasibility study concluded

133
00:08:46,624 --> 00:08:50,194
that the nuclear technologies
then available were

134
00:08:50,274 --> 00:08:53,775
"not yet suitable for
deployment in Singapore."

135
00:08:53,775 --> 00:08:58,013
Two years later the Singapore
Nuclear Research and Safety

136
00:08:58,093 --> 00:09:02,180
Initiative was set up to
build expertise, and more than

137
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:06,275
150 million dollars has gone
into its research since.

138
00:09:06,275 --> 00:09:10,448
Singapore has signed agreements
with the United States, France,

139
00:09:10,528 --> 00:09:14,431
the United Arab Emirates and
Sweden to build up its ability

140
00:09:14,511 --> 00:09:18,617
to assess nuclear power, and it
is watching the development of

141
00:09:18,697 --> 00:09:22,950
small modular reactors, a newer,
smaller kind of nuclear plant.

142
00:09:22,950 --> 00:09:26,184
The government says it
has not yet made any

143
00:09:26,264 --> 00:09:29,500
decision on whether to use nuclear energy.

144
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:33,237
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has always

145
00:09:33,317 --> 00:09:36,567
imported its fuel, from
the coal of the colonial

146
00:09:36,647 --> 00:09:40,325
era to the oil of the PUB
years and the gas of today.

147
00:09:40,325 --> 00:09:44,998
What changed over those three
decades was not the dependence

148
00:09:45,078 --> 00:09:49,038
but the number of ways in,
from one pipe in 1992 to

149
00:09:49,118 --> 00:09:53,475
three by 2003, and then to
ships from around the world.

150
00:09:53,475 --> 00:09:58,030
That is why a cut that would
once have been a crisis

151
00:09:58,110 --> 00:10:02,300
came to less than 2% of
a day's demand in 2026.
